actly the same order as sequential because sequential also adds j=0,1,...,n-1 sequentially. So no reordering across chunks. The only difference is that we might be adding contributions from different i's interleaved in time, but each i's own accumulation sequence remains unchanged. Therefore tiling both loops with proper ordering preserves exact floating-point results.\n\nThus we can implement a blocked version: choose block sizes for i and j (e.g., IBLOCK=4, JBLOCK=128). Use SoA layout to improve spatial locality when accessing body coordinates in the inner loop. Also use OpenMP parallelization over i-blocks (each thread processes one or more i-blocks sequentially within its chunk). This should combine benefits of tiling for cache reuse and parallelism.\n\nWe need to ensure no VLA issues: define block sizes as macros, allocate local arrays with fixed size using those macros (e.g., float fx[IBLOCK] = {0.0f}; but that's still a variable-length array if IBLOCK is not constant? Actually #define IBLOCK 4 makes it compile-time constant, so no VLA issue. The previous failure was because they used const int or something? They had error: variable-sized object may not be initialized. That suggests they declared arrays with size that was not a compile-time constant (maybe using a parameter). So we must use #define constants.\n\nImplementation plan:\n- Change data layout to Structure-of-Arrays: store positions and velocities in separate arrays of floats, e.g., float *x, *y, *z, *vx, *vy, *vz. Allocate with posix_memalign for alignment.\n- In body_force, use tiling over i (outer loop) and j (inner loop). For each i-block (size IBLOCK), we have local accumulators fx[IBLOCK], fy[IBLOCK], fz[IBLOCK] initialized to zero. Then iterate over j-blocks of size JBLOCK. Inside a j-block, load the coordinates of all bodies in that block into local arrays? Actually for each j in the block, compute dx,dy,dz relative to each i in the current i-block and accumulate. To maximize reuse, we can p